var FLASHCARDS = [

/* ---------- Complexity & Analysis ---------- */
{id:"cx01",deck:"complexity",q:"Average and worst-case time for a hash map lookup — and what causes the worst case?",a:"`O(1)` average, `O(n)` worst. The worst case happens when every key collides into one bucket, degrading it to a linked scan. Java 8+ converts long buckets to trees, capping it at `O(log n)`."},
{id:"cx02",deck:"complexity",q:"Cost of building a heap from n unsorted items?",a:"`O(n)` using bottom-up heapify — NOT `O(n log n)`. The sum of node heights telescopes to less than n. Pushing n items one at a time IS `O(n log n)`."},
{id:"cx03",deck:"complexity",q:"Quickselect: average and worst-case complexity?",a:"`O(n)` average (n + n/2 + n/4 + ... = 2n), `O(n^2)` worst on adversarial pivots. Median-of-medians makes the worst case `O(n)` but with a large constant."},
{id:"cx04",deck:"complexity",q:"Why is appending to a dynamic array O(1) amortised?",a:"Capacity doubles on overflow, so total copy work across n appends is 1+2+4+...+n < 2n. Averaged over n operations that is `O(1)` each, even though one individual append is `O(n)`."},
{id:"cx05",deck:"complexity",q:"BFS and DFS complexity on a graph — for both representations?",a:"Adjacency list: `O(V + E)` time, `O(V)` space. Adjacency matrix: `O(V^2)` time, because you scan a full row per vertex regardless of degree."},
{id:"cx06",deck:"complexity",q:"Dijkstra's complexity with each priority-queue choice?",a:"Binary heap: `O((V + E) log V)`. Fibonacci heap: `O(E + V log V)`. Plain array scan: `O(V^2)` — which actually wins on dense graphs where E approaches V^2."},
{id:"cx07",deck:"complexity",q:"What is the lower bound for comparison-based sorting, and why?",a:"Omega(n log n). The decision tree must have n! leaves to distinguish every permutation, so its height is at least log2(n!) which is Theta(n log n). Counting and radix sort beat it only by not comparing."},
{id:"cx08",deck:"complexity",q:"Merge sort versus quicksort — state four differences.",a:"Merge: `O(n log n)` guaranteed, stable, needs `O(n)` extra space. Quick: `O(n log n)` average but `O(n^2)` worst, unstable, in-place, and usually faster in practice due to cache locality."},
{id:"cx09",deck:"complexity",q:"Union-Find with path compression AND union by rank — amortised complexity?",a:"`O(alpha(n))` per operation, where alpha is the inverse Ackermann function — below 5 for any n you will ever see. Treat it as effectively constant. Only ONE of the two optimisations gives `O(log n)`."},
{id:"cx10",deck:"complexity",q:"Trie: time and space complexity of insert and search?",a:"Both `O(L)` where L is the word length — completely independent of how many words are stored. Space is `O(total characters * alphabet)` worst case with array nodes, much less with map nodes."},
{id:"cx11",deck:"complexity",q:"Segment tree: build, query, update, and space?",a:"Build `O(n)`, query `O(log n)`, point update `O(log n)`, space `O(4n)` for the array representation. Range updates need lazy propagation to stay `O(log n)`."},
{id:"cx12",deck:"complexity",q:"What is the difference between amortised and average complexity?",a:"Amortised is a guarantee over a worst-case SEQUENCE of operations — no probability involved. Average is an expectation over a distribution of inputs. Dynamic array append is amortised `O(1)`; hash lookup is average `O(1)`."},
{id:"cx13",deck:"complexity",q:"Why is repeated string concatenation in a loop O(n^2)?",a:"Strings are immutable in Java, Python and JS, so each concatenation copies the whole accumulated string. Use a list plus join, or a StringBuilder, for `O(n)`."},
{id:"cx14",deck:"complexity",q:"Master theorem: solve T(n) = 2T(n/2) + O(n).",a:"a=2, b=2, so n^(log_b a) = n^1 = n, which matches f(n) = n. That is case 2, giving `O(n log n)` — the merge sort recurrence."},
{id:"cx15",deck:"complexity",q:"Complexity of generating all subsets, and all permutations?",a:"Subsets: `O(n * 2^n)` — there are 2^n subsets and copying each costs `O(n)`. Permutations: `O(n * n!)`. Both are output-bound, so you cannot do better."},
{id:"cx16",deck:"complexity",q:"How do you reverse-engineer the intended complexity from the constraints?",a:"Budget roughly 10^8 simple operations per second. n <= 10 suggests `O(n!)`, n <= 20 bitmask `O(2^n)`, n <= 500 `O(n^3)`, n <= 5000 `O(n^2)`, n <= 10^5 `O(n log n)`, n <= 10^7 `O(n)`."},
{id:"cx17",deck:"complexity",q:"What is the complexity of binary searching the ANSWER space?",a:"`O(log(range) * cost_of_check)`. The log is over the numeric span of possible answers, not over n — so a range of 10^9 costs only about 30 iterations."},
{id:"cx18",deck:"complexity",q:"What space does recursion actually cost?",a:"`O(depth)` stack frames. DFS on a graph is `O(V)` worst case; on a balanced tree `O(log n)`; on a degenerate tree `O(n)`. Always state recursion stack separately from auxiliary data."},
{id:"cx19",deck:"complexity",q:"When you say O(1) space but the output is size n, what do you mean?",a:"You mean `O(1)` AUXILIARY space — excluding the space required to hold the mandated output. Say the word auxiliary explicitly; interviewers listen for it."},
{id:"cx20",deck:"complexity",q:"Nested loops where j starts at i and k starts at j — what is the complexity?",a:"Still `O(n^3)`. Shrinking bounds only change the constant: the count is n choose 3 which is n^3/6. Constants never change the class."},
{id:"cx21",deck:"complexity",q:"Complexity of `x in collection` for a list versus a set in Python?",a:"List is `O(n)` (linear scan), set and dict are `O(1)` average. Using `in` on a list inside a loop is the single most common accidental `O(n^2)`."},
{id:"cx22",deck:"complexity",q:"What is the complexity of sorting followed by a single linear pass?",a:"`O(n log n)` — the sort dominates. This is why 'sort first' is almost always an acceptable opening answer, and why beating it requires a hash map or a heap."},

/* ---------- Pattern Recognition ---------- */
{id:"pt01",deck:"patterns",q:"SIGNAL: longest contiguous subarray satisfying a property, all values positive.",a:"Sliding window. Expand right always; shrink left while the window is invalid; record the answer after expanding."},
{id:"pt02",deck:"patterns",q:"SIGNAL: count subarrays whose sum equals K, values may be negative.",a:"Prefix sum plus hash map of prefix counts. Sliding window fails here because the window is no longer monotone. Seed the map with `{0: 1}`."},
{id:"pt03",deck:"patterns",q:"SIGNAL: sorted array, find a pair summing to a target.",a:"Two pointers from both ends. If the sum is too small move left rightward, too large move right leftward. `O(n)` time, `O(1)` space."},
{id:"pt04",deck:"patterns",q:"SIGNAL: minimise the maximum, or maximise the minimum.",a:"Binary search on the answer. Define `can(x)` as a monotone feasibility check, then binary search for the boundary where it flips."},
{id:"pt05",deck:"patterns",q:"SIGNAL: for each element, find the next greater element.",a:"Monotonic decreasing stack of indices. Pop while the current value exceeds the stack top; the current element is the answer for each popped index."},
{id:"pt06",deck:"patterns",q:"SIGNAL: top K frequent elements.",a:"Two answers: size-K min-heap for `O(n log k)`, or bucket sort indexed by frequency for `O(n)`. Offer the heap, then mention the bucket version — that is the impressive one."},
{id:"pt07",deck:"patterns",q:"SIGNAL: return every valid configuration or arrangement.",a:"Backtracking. Choose, recurse, un-choose. Sort the input first if duplicates must be suppressed."},
{id:"pt08",deck:"patterns",q:"SIGNAL: count the number of ways, and the brute force re-solves subproblems.",a:"Dynamic programming. Write the recurrence in words, memoise the recursion first, then convert to a bottom-up table if space matters."},
{id:"pt09",deck:"patterns",q:"SIGNAL: courses with prerequisites, or a build order.",a:"Topological sort. Kahn's BFS with in-degrees is easier to reason about, and any leftover nodes at the end prove a cycle."},
{id:"pt10",deck:"patterns",q:"SIGNAL: repeatedly asked whether two elements are in the same group.",a:"Union-Find (disjoint set union) with path compression and union by rank."},
{id:"pt11",deck:"patterns",q:"SIGNAL: shortest path where every edge costs the same.",a:"Plain BFS. Do NOT reach for Dijkstra — BFS is `O(V + E)` and Dijkstra adds a needless log factor."},
{id:"pt12",deck:"patterns",q:"SIGNAL: shortest path with non-negative weights, versus with negative weights.",a:"Non-negative: Dijkstra with a min-heap. Negative allowed: Bellman-Ford (V-1 relaxation rounds, a Vth round detects a negative cycle)."},
{id:"pt13",deck:"patterns",q:"SIGNAL: detect a cycle — how does it differ between undirected and directed graphs?",a:"Undirected: DFS tracking the parent, or union-find (an edge inside one set is a cycle). Directed: DFS with three colours, or Kahn's algorithm leaving nodes unprocessed."},
{id:"pt14",deck:"patterns",q:"SIGNAL: longest path in a DAG.",a:"Topological order plus DP along that order. Dijkstra does not solve longest path; longest path in a general graph is NP-hard, the DAG structure is what saves you."},
{id:"pt15",deck:"patterns",q:"SIGNAL: median of a stream of numbers.",a:"Two heaps — a max-heap of the lower half and a min-heap of the upper half, rebalanced so their sizes differ by at most one."},
{id:"pt16",deck:"patterns",q:"SIGNAL: many words queried by prefix.",a:"Trie. `O(L)` per query independent of dictionary size, and it supports wildcard search by branching over all children."},
{id:"pt17",deck:"patterns",q:"SIGNAL: every element appears twice except one.",a:"XOR everything. a XOR a = 0 and a XOR 0 = a, so the pairs cancel. `O(n)` time and `O(1)` space with no hash map."},
{id:"pt18",deck:"patterns",q:"SIGNAL: merge overlapping intervals versus maximum non-overlapping intervals.",a:"Merge: sort by START, then extend or push. Maximum non-overlapping: sort by END and greedily take — earliest finish leaves the most room."},
{id:"pt19",deck:"patterns",q:"SIGNAL: count palindromic substrings.",a:"Expand around each of the 2n-1 centres for `O(n^2)` time and `O(1)` space. The DP-on-(l,r) version is also `O(n^2)` but costs `O(n^2)` space."},
{id:"pt20",deck:"patterns",q:"SIGNAL: find where a linked-list cycle begins.",a:"Floyd's tortoise and hare. After they meet, reset one pointer to the head and advance both one step at a time — they meet at the cycle entrance."},
{id:"pt21",deck:"patterns",q:"SIGNAL: compare two strings as SUBSEQUENCES rather than substrings.",a:"2-D DP over (i, j) prefixes. Substring problems get sliding windows or expand-around-centre; subsequence problems get a table."},
{id:"pt22",deck:"patterns",q:"SIGNAL: array of length n containing values in the range 1..n.",a:"Index-as-hash. Either negate the value at index `abs(v)-1` to mark it seen, or cyclic-swap each value into its home position. Gives `O(1)` auxiliary space."},
{id:"pt23",deck:"patterns",q:"SIGNAL: grid where movement costs vary per cell.",a:"Dijkstra on the implicit grid graph, or binary search on the threshold plus a BFS/DSU feasibility check (Path with Minimum Effort, Swim in Rising Water)."},
{id:"pt24",deck:"patterns",q:"SIGNAL: longest streak of consecutive days or IDs.",a:"Gaps and islands. Group by (value minus row number / index) — rows in the same run share that key. In SQL this is the canonical streak query."},
{id:"pt25",deck:"patterns",q:"SIGNAL: pick items subject to a capacity or exact target.",a:"Knapsack DP. 0/1 if each item is used once (reverse the capacity loop in the 1-D version), unbounded if unlimited (forward loop)."},
{id:"pt26",deck:"patterns",q:"SIGNAL: spread from several starting points simultaneously.",a:"Multi-source BFS — push ALL sources into the queue before the first pop. Rotting Oranges and Walls and Gates are the canonical examples."},
{id:"pt27",deck:"patterns",q:"SIGNAL: maximum in every window of fixed size k.",a:"Monotonic deque holding indices in decreasing value order. Pop from the back while smaller, pop from the front when out of window. `O(n)` total."},
{id:"pt28",deck:"patterns",q:"SIGNAL: the interviewer says 'can you do it in one pass with constant space'.",a:"They are pointing at two pointers, Boyer-Moore majority vote, Kadane, XOR, or index-as-hash. Enumerate those five out loud and one will fit."},

/* ---------- Data Structures ---------- */
{id:"ds01",deck:"structures",q:"Array versus linked list — compare on four axes.",a:"Random access: `O(1)` vs `O(n)`. Insert at a known position: `O(n)` vs `O(1)`. Memory: contiguous and cache-friendly vs scattered with per-node pointer overhead. Resize: amortised copy vs free."},
{id:"ds02",deck:"structures",q:"How does a hash map resize, and what does it cost?",a:"When load factor exceeds a threshold (often 0.75) it allocates a table roughly twice as large and rehashes every key. That one operation is `O(n)`, but amortised over insertions it stays `O(1)`."},
{id:"ds03",deck:"structures",q:"Two strategies for handling hash collisions.",a:"Separate chaining — each bucket holds a list or tree. Open addressing — probe for the next free slot (linear, quadratic, or double hashing). Chaining tolerates high load factors better; open addressing is more cache-friendly."},
{id:"ds04",deck:"structures",q:"State the heap invariant and the array index arithmetic.",a:"A complete binary tree where every parent is <= both children (min-heap). Stored in an array: children of i are at 2i+1 and 2i+2, parent of i is at (i-1)/2."},
{id:"ds05",deck:"structures",q:"Why is a heap not a sorted array?",a:"Only the root is guaranteed to be the extreme element. Siblings have no defined order. Getting sorted output requires n extractions, which is exactly heapsort at `O(n log n)`."},
{id:"ds06",deck:"structures",q:"When is a BST better than a hash map?",a:"Whenever ORDER matters: range queries, successor and predecessor, kth smallest, and sorted iteration — all `O(log n)`. A hash map gives `O(1)` point lookup but no ordering at all."},
{id:"ds07",deck:"structures",q:"State the five red-black tree properties.",a:"Every node is red or black; the root is black; all leaves (NIL) are black; a red node has no red child; every root-to-leaf path contains the same number of black nodes. Together these bound the height at 2*log(n+1)."},
{id:"ds08",deck:"structures",q:"AVL versus red-black tree — when does each win?",a:"AVL is more strictly balanced (heights differ by at most 1), so lookups are faster but rebalancing is more frequent. Red-black tolerates more imbalance and rebalances less, so it is better for write-heavy workloads. Most standard libraries choose red-black."},
{id:"ds09",deck:"structures",q:"What are the two Union-Find optimisations and what does each do?",a:"Path compression flattens the tree during find by repointing every node to the root. Union by rank/size always attaches the shallower tree to the deeper one. Both together give inverse-Ackermann amortised time."},
{id:"ds10",deck:"structures",q:"Which two structures combine to make an LRU cache, and why each?",a:"A hash map for `O(1)` lookup by key, plus a doubly linked list for `O(1)` move-to-front and `O(1)` eviction from the tail. The map stores key to node pointer. A singly linked list cannot unlink in `O(1)`."},
{id:"ds11",deck:"structures",q:"What is a Bloom filter and what is its one-sided error?",a:"A probabilistic set using k hash functions over a bit array. False positives are possible, false negatives are NOT. Used as a cheap pre-filter before an expensive lookup. Standard Bloom filters cannot delete."},
{id:"ds12",deck:"structures",q:"Adjacency list versus adjacency matrix.",a:"List: `O(V + E)` space, iterate neighbours in `O(deg)`, edge-exists check `O(deg)`. Matrix: `O(V^2)` space, edge-exists in `O(1)`, but neighbour iteration is always `O(V)`. Use a matrix only for dense graphs or when you constantly test edge existence."},
{id:"ds13",deck:"structures",q:"What invariant does a monotonic stack maintain, and what does it buy you?",a:"Values in the stack stay sorted (increasing or decreasing). Each element is pushed and popped at most once, so the whole scan is `O(n)` — that is how next-greater and histogram problems become linear."},
{id:"ds14",deck:"structures",q:"Segment tree versus Binary Indexed Tree — which and when?",a:"BIT is smaller, faster, and simpler but essentially only handles prefix-reducible operations such as sums. A segment tree handles arbitrary associative merges (min, max, gcd, custom) and supports lazy range updates."},
{id:"ds15",deck:"structures",q:"How does a trie node trade memory against speed?",a:"A fixed 26-entry array per node gives `O(1)` child access but wastes memory on sparse branches. A hash map per node saves memory at the cost of hashing. For large alphabets, always use the map."},
{id:"ds16",deck:"structures",q:"What is a skip list and why does it exist?",a:"A probabilistic layered linked list giving `O(log n)` expected search, insert and delete. It is far simpler to implement lock-free than a balanced tree, which is why Redis uses it for sorted sets."},
{id:"ds17",deck:"structures",q:"Deque — what does it give you that a stack or queue does not?",a:"`O(1)` insert and remove at BOTH ends. That is exactly what sliding-window-maximum needs: append at the back, evict stale indices from the front."},
{id:"ds18",deck:"structures",q:"What does string immutability imply for your algorithms?",a:"Every modification allocates a new string. Building character by character in a loop is `O(n^2)`. Slicing is `O(k)` not `O(1)`. Use a mutable buffer, and prefer index ranges over repeated slicing."},
{id:"ds19",deck:"structures",q:"When would you choose a circular buffer?",a:"Fixed-capacity streaming: last N events, a producer-consumer ring, or a rate-limiter window. It gives `O(1)` push and pop with zero allocation after construction."},
{id:"ds20",deck:"structures",q:"How do you get O(1) insert, delete and getRandom in one structure?",a:"An array of values plus a hash map from value to its index. To delete, swap the target with the last element, fix that element's index in the map, then pop. getRandom picks a random array index."},
{id:"ds21",deck:"structures",q:"What is a self-balancing tree's height guarantee and why does it matter?",a:"`O(log n)`. Without balancing, inserting sorted data degenerates the BST into a linked list, turning every `O(log n)` operation into `O(n)` — a common trick question."},
{id:"ds22",deck:"structures",q:"Priority queue versus keeping a sorted list.",a:"Heap: insert `O(log n)`, peek-min `O(1)`, extract-min `O(log n)`, but no ordered iteration. Sorted list: `O(n)` insert, `O(1)` peek, and full ordering. If you only ever need the extreme, the heap wins."},

/* ---------- Named Algorithms ---------- */
{id:"al01",deck:"algos",q:"Write the lower-bound binary search invariant that never off-by-ones.",a:"Use `lo = 0, hi = n` with `while (lo < hi)`, `mid = lo + (hi-lo)/2`, and on a true predicate `hi = mid` else `lo = mid + 1`. At exit lo is the first true index. One template, memorised, forever."},
{id:"al02",deck:"algos",q:"Why does resetting one pointer to the head find the cycle entrance in Floyd's algorithm?",a:"Let the tail length be a and the meeting point be b into a cycle of length c. The fast pointer has travelled twice the slow pointer, and the algebra gives a = c - b (mod c). So walking a steps from the head and a steps from the meeting point lands on the same node."},
{id:"al03",deck:"algos",q:"State Kadane's algorithm in one line and its key insight.",a:"`cur = max(x, cur + x); best = max(best, cur)`. The insight: a running sum that has gone negative can never help a future subarray, so discard it and start fresh."},
{id:"al04",deck:"algos",q:"Describe the Dutch National Flag partition.",a:"Three pointers low, mid, high. If `a[mid]` is 0 swap with low and advance both; if 1 just advance mid; if 2 swap with high and DECREMENT high without advancing mid — because the swapped-in value is unexamined. Sorts three values in one pass."},
{id:"al05",deck:"algos",q:"Kahn's algorithm for topological sort — outline it.",a:"Compute in-degrees, enqueue every zero in-degree node, then repeatedly pop, append to the order, and decrement each neighbour's in-degree, enqueuing any that hit zero. If the output length is less than V, the graph has a cycle."},
{id:"al06",deck:"algos",q:"Dijkstra — outline it and name the one thing people forget.",a:"Push (0, source). Pop the smallest distance; if it is stale (greater than the recorded best) SKIP IT; otherwise relax every neighbour and push improvements. The stale-entry skip is the step people forget, and it is what makes lazy deletion correct."},
{id:"al07",deck:"algos",q:"Bellman-Ford — how many rounds, and how do you detect a negative cycle?",a:"Relax all E edges V-1 times, because any shortest path uses at most V-1 edges. Then run a Vth round: if anything still improves, a negative cycle is reachable."},
{id:"al08",deck:"algos",q:"Floyd-Warshall — write the loop nesting and explain why the order matters.",a:"`for k: for i: for j: d[i][j] = min(d[i][j], d[i][k] + d[k][j])`. k MUST be outermost because it represents 'all intermediate vertices considered so far'. Any other order produces wrong answers."},
{id:"al09",deck:"algos",q:"Kruskal versus Prim for MST — when does each fit?",a:"Kruskal sorts all edges and unions with DSU — `O(E log E)`, better for sparse graphs. Prim grows one tree with a heap — `O(E log V)`, better for dense graphs. Both rely on the cut property."},
{id:"al10",deck:"algos",q:"What does the KMP failure/LPS array store, and why does it help?",a:"For each prefix, the length of the longest proper prefix that is also a suffix. On a mismatch you jump the pattern forward by that amount instead of restarting, so the text pointer never moves backwards — giving `O(n + m)`."},
{id:"al11",deck:"algos",q:"Rabin-Karp — what is the trick and what is the risk?",a:"A rolling hash lets you update the window hash in `O(1)` by removing the leading character and adding the trailing one. The risk is hash collisions, so you must verify a candidate match character by character."},
{id:"al12",deck:"algos",q:"Sieve of Eratosthenes — complexity and the two implementation details.",a:"`O(n log log n)`. Start marking from `i*i` (smaller multiples already have a smaller factor), and only loop i while `i*i <= n`."},
{id:"al13",deck:"algos",q:"Fast exponentiation — state the recurrence.",a:"`pow(x, n)` = pow(x*x, n/2) when n is even, and x * pow(x, n-1) when odd. `O(log n)` multiplications. Handle negative n by inverting, and take the modulus at every step to avoid overflow."},
{id:"al14",deck:"algos",q:"Boyer-Moore majority vote — how does it work?",a:"Keep a candidate and a count. Matching increments, mismatching decrements, and a zero count adopts the current element as the new candidate. Works only because a true majority (> n/2) survives all cancellation. Verify with a second pass if the majority is not guaranteed."},
{id:"al15",deck:"algos",q:"Morris inorder traversal — what does it achieve and how?",a:"Inorder traversal in `O(1)` space by temporarily threading each node's inorder predecessor's right pointer back to the node, then unthreading on the second visit. Trades pointer mutation for stack space."},
{id:"al16",deck:"algos",q:"Fisher-Yates shuffle — write it and name the classic bug.",a:"Iterate i from n-1 down to 1, pick `j` uniformly in `[0, i]`, swap. The bug is picking j from the full range `[0, n-1]` every time, which produces a biased distribution."},
{id:"al17",deck:"algos",q:"Reservoir sampling — how do you pick k items from a stream of unknown length?",a:"Keep the first k. For the i-th item (i > k), accept it with probability k/i and evict a uniformly random one of the k. Every item ends with probability k/n."},
{id:"al18",deck:"algos",q:"Quickselect — describe the algorithm.",a:"Partition around a pivot as in quicksort, then recurse into ONLY the side containing the kth index. Because you discard half each time on average, the recurrence is n + n/2 + n/4 ... giving `O(n)`."},
{id:"al19",deck:"algos",q:"Give the backtracking skeleton in four lines.",a:"`if (goal) { record(copy of path); return; }` then `for each choice: if (!valid) continue; apply(choice); recurse(); undo(choice);`. The copy and the undo are the two lines candidates forget."},
{id:"al20",deck:"algos",q:"Memoisation versus tabulation — trade-offs.",a:"Memo (top-down) is closer to the recurrence, only visits reachable states, but costs recursion stack. Tabulation (bottom-up) has no stack risk and enables rolling-array space optimisation, but computes every state and needs a correct iteration order."},
{id:"al21",deck:"algos",q:"Euclid's GCD — state it and the LCM relationship.",a:"`gcd(a, b) = gcd(b, a mod b)`, terminating when b is 0. `lcm(a, b) = a / gcd(a, b) * b` — divide BEFORE multiplying to avoid overflow."},
{id:"al22",deck:"algos",q:"Name four bit tricks and what each does.",a:"`n & (n-1)` clears the lowest set bit (and tests power-of-two when the result is 0). `n & -n` isolates the lowest set bit. `n ^ n = 0`. `x >> 1` halves, `x << 1` doubles. Iterating subsets of a mask: `for (s = m; s; s = (s-1) & m)`."},
{id:"al23",deck:"algos",q:"How do you merge k sorted lists efficiently, and what is the cost?",a:"Min-heap of the k current heads: pop the smallest, push its successor. `O(N log k)` where N is the total element count. The alternative is pairwise divide-and-conquer merging, which is the same complexity."},
{id:"al24",deck:"algos",q:"How do you find the lowest common ancestor in a general binary tree?",a:"Recurse; if the node is null or equals either target, return it. If both subtree calls return non-null, this node is the LCA; otherwise propagate whichever side is non-null. `O(n)` single pass."},

/* ---------- Traps & Edge Cases ---------- */
{id:"tr01",deck:"traps",q:"Why is `mid = (lo + hi) / 2` dangerous?",a:"It overflows when lo + hi exceeds the integer maximum. Use `lo + (hi - lo) / 2`. This is a genuine historic bug that sat in the JDK binary search for nine years."},
{id:"tr02",deck:"traps",q:"What is wrong with `def f(x, acc=[])` in Python?",a:"The default list is created ONCE at function definition and shared across every call, so results accumulate across invocations. Use `acc=None` and initialise inside the body."},
{id:"tr03",deck:"traps",q:"What does `[[0]*n]*m` create in Python?",a:"m references to the SAME row. Writing to `grid[0][0]` changes every row. Use `[[0]*n for _ in range(m)]`."},
{id:"tr04",deck:"traps",q:"Why does appending `path` in backtracking give you a list of empty lists?",a:"You appended a REFERENCE to the mutable path, which gets emptied as the recursion unwinds. Append a copy: `res.append(path[:])` or `new ArrayList<>(path)`."},
{id:"tr05",deck:"traps",q:"What breaks when you modify a collection while iterating over it?",a:"Skipped elements or a ConcurrentModificationException. Iterate over a copy, build a new collection, or iterate indices in reverse when deleting."},
{id:"tr06",deck:"traps",q:"In Java, why does `s1 == s2` sometimes work for strings and sometimes not?",a:"`==` compares references. String literals are interned so they may share an object, but runtime-constructed strings are distinct. Always use `.equals()`."},
{id:"tr07",deck:"traps",q:"What does `-7 % 3` give, and why does it matter?",a:"-1 in Java, C and JavaScript; 2 in Python. Any hashing or circular-index arithmetic on possibly-negative values must normalise: `((x % m) + m) % m`."},
{id:"tr08",deck:"traps",q:"Which edge cases should you name out loud before writing any code?",a:"Empty input, single element, all elements identical, already sorted, reverse sorted, negative numbers, integer bounds, and duplicates. Naming them earns marks even when they do not apply."},
{id:"tr09",deck:"traps",q:"Binary search with duplicates — what goes wrong?",a:"A plain binary search returns an arbitrary matching index. If you need the first or last occurrence you must keep searching after a match — collapse `hi = mid` for the first, or `lo = mid + 1` for the last."},
{id:"tr10",deck:"traps",q:"Why can you not compare floats with `==`?",a:"Binary floating point cannot represent most decimals exactly, so 0.1 + 0.2 does not equal 0.3. Compare with an epsilon, or work in integers by scaling."},
{id:"tr11",deck:"traps",q:"What happens to your solution when the recursion depth is 10^5?",a:"Stack overflow — Python's default limit is 1000 and the JVM typically dies around a few thousand frames. Convert to an explicit stack, or state the risk out loud and note that iterative DFS avoids it."},
{id:"tr12",deck:"traps",q:"When does a LEFT JOIN silently become an INNER JOIN?",a:"When you put a condition on the right-hand table in the WHERE clause. Unmatched rows have NULL there, the predicate evaluates to NULL, and they are filtered out. Move the condition into the ON clause."},
{id:"tr13",deck:"traps",q:"Your two-pointer solution passes the samples but fails on duplicates. What is missing?",a:"The duplicate skip. After recording a valid triple, advance past every repeat of both pointer values, and skip repeated values of the outer loop index with `if (i > 0 && a[i] == a[i-1]) continue;`."},
{id:"tr14",deck:"traps",q:"What is the most common bug in a sliding-window shortest-window problem?",a:"Recording the answer at the wrong moment. For SHORTEST you must record inside the shrink loop while the window is still valid; for LONGEST you record after expanding. Getting this backwards passes the samples and fails the hidden tests."},
{id:"tr15",deck:"traps",q:"Why does your BFS run out of memory on a large grid?",a:"You marked cells visited on DEQUEUE instead of on ENQUEUE, so the same cell gets pushed once per neighbour. Mark visited the moment you push."},
{id:"tr16",deck:"traps",q:"You return the right answer locally but the judge disagrees. What state should you suspect?",a:"Module-level or static mutable state — a memo dictionary, a global result list, or a visited set that was not reset between test cases."},

/* ---------- SQL Core ---------- */
{id:"sq01",deck:"sqlcore",q:"State the LOGICAL execution order of a SELECT statement.",a:"FROM and JOIN, then WHERE, then GROUP BY, then HAVING, then window functions, then SELECT, then DISTINCT, then ORDER BY, then LIMIT/OFFSET. Every alias rule follows from this order."},
{id:"sq02",deck:"sqlcore",q:"Why can ORDER BY use a SELECT alias but WHERE cannot?",a:"WHERE is evaluated BEFORE SELECT, so the alias does not exist yet. ORDER BY runs after SELECT, so it does. Same reason HAVING can reference an aggregate and WHERE cannot."},
{id:"sq03",deck:"sqlcore",q:"WHERE versus HAVING in one sentence each.",a:"WHERE filters individual ROWS before grouping. HAVING filters GROUPS after aggregation. If your condition mentions an aggregate function, it belongs in HAVING."},
{id:"sq04",deck:"sqlcore",q:"COUNT(*), COUNT(col), COUNT(DISTINCT col) — how do they differ?",a:"COUNT(*) counts every row including all-NULL ones. COUNT(col) skips rows where col is NULL. COUNT(DISTINCT col) skips NULLs AND collapses repeats."},
{id:"sq05",deck:"sqlcore",q:"Why is NULL = NULL not TRUE?",a:"NULL means unknown, and two unknowns cannot be shown equal, so the comparison yields NULL — which is not TRUE, so the row is filtered out. Use `IS NULL`, or `IS NOT DISTINCT FROM` for null-safe equality."},
{id:"sq06",deck:"sqlcore",q:"Why can NOT IN return zero rows unexpectedly?",a:"If the subquery yields even one NULL, then `x NOT IN (...)` evaluates to NULL for every x — never TRUE — so nothing is returned. Use NOT EXISTS, which is null-safe."},
{id:"sq07",deck:"sqlcore",q:"Give the truth table for AND and OR with NULL.",a:"`TRUE AND NULL` = NULL. `FALSE AND NULL` = FALSE. `TRUE OR NULL` = TRUE. `FALSE OR NULL` = NULL. NULL short-circuits only when the other operand already decides the result."},
{id:"sq08",deck:"sqlcore",q:"Name the join types and what each keeps.",a:"INNER keeps matches only. LEFT keeps all left rows. RIGHT keeps all right rows. FULL OUTER keeps both sides. CROSS is the Cartesian product. SELF is a table joined to itself under two aliases."},
{id:"sq09",deck:"sqlcore",q:"What is an anti-join, and give three ways to write one.",a:"Rows in A with no match in B. Write it as `NOT EXISTS (correlated subquery)`, or `LEFT JOIN ... WHERE b.key IS NULL`, or `EXCEPT`. NOT EXISTS is generally the safest and often the fastest."},
{id:"sq10",deck:"sqlcore",q:"Why does joining on a non-unique key give wrong SUM totals?",a:"The join multiplies rows — one left row matching three right rows becomes three rows, so its value is counted three times. Aggregate the many-side to one row per key in a CTE first, THEN join."},
{id:"sq11",deck:"sqlcore",q:"EXISTS versus IN — when does it matter?",a:"EXISTS short-circuits at the first match and is null-safe. IN materialises the list and breaks under NOT IN with NULLs. For large subqueries EXISTS usually plans better; for small constant lists IN is clearer."},
{id:"sq12",deck:"sqlcore",q:"UNION versus UNION ALL.",a:"UNION removes duplicates, which requires a sort or hash and therefore costs real time. UNION ALL just concatenates. Default to UNION ALL unless you genuinely need deduplication."},
{id:"sq13",deck:"sqlcore",q:"What is a correlated subquery and what does it cost?",a:"A subquery that references a column from the outer query, so it is conceptually re-evaluated per outer row — potentially `O(n*m)`. A window function or a pre-aggregated join usually replaces it."},
{id:"sq14",deck:"sqlcore",q:"COALESCE versus NULLIF versus IFNULL.",a:"COALESCE returns the first non-NULL of its arguments (standard, variadic). NULLIF(a, b) returns NULL when a equals b — handy to guard division by zero. IFNULL is the MySQL two-argument shorthand and is not portable."},
{id:"sq15",deck:"sqlcore",q:"Write the shape of a query that finds duplicates.",a:"`SELECT key, COUNT(*) FROM t GROUP BY key HAVING COUNT(*) > 1`. To delete them, keep the minimum id per key or use `ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY key ORDER BY id)` and delete where the number is above 1."},
{id:"sq16",deck:"sqlcore",q:"Does DISTINCT apply to just the first column?",a:"No — it applies to the ENTIRE select list as a tuple. `SELECT DISTINCT a, b` dedupes on the pair. To dedupe on one column while showing others, use a window function or GROUP BY."},
{id:"sq17",deck:"sqlcore",q:"What is the difference between DELETE, TRUNCATE and DROP?",a:"DELETE is DML: row by row, fires triggers, logged, rollback-able, can have a WHERE. TRUNCATE is DDL: deallocates pages, much faster, no WHERE, no row triggers, resets identity, often not rollback-able. DROP removes the table definition entirely."},
{id:"sq18",deck:"sqlcore",q:"Why is `SELECT *` discouraged in production queries?",a:"It breaks when columns are added or reordered, transfers unnecessary bytes, prevents the planner from using a covering index, and hides intent in code review."},
{id:"sq19",deck:"sqlcore",q:"Write the shape of a conditional-aggregation pivot.",a:"`SELECT id, SUM(CASE WHEN type = 'A' THEN amt ELSE 0 END) AS a, SUM(CASE WHEN type = 'B' THEN amt ELSE 0 END) AS b FROM t GROUP BY id`. Portable across every dialect, unlike PIVOT syntax."},
{id:"sq20",deck:"sqlcore",q:"COUNT(CASE WHEN c THEN 1 END) versus COUNT(CASE WHEN c THEN 1 ELSE 0 END).",a:"The first counts only matching rows, because the implicit ELSE NULL is skipped by COUNT. The second counts EVERY row, because 0 is not NULL. This single mistake breaks many pivot queries."},
{id:"sq21",deck:"sqlcore",q:"How do you compute a rate or percentage safely?",a:"Use `AVG(CASE WHEN c THEN 1.0 ELSE 0 END)`, or cast before dividing. Watch integer division truncating to 0, and guard the denominator with NULLIF to avoid divide-by-zero."},
{id:"sq22",deck:"sqlcore",q:"Why does wrapping a column in a function make a query slow?",a:"It makes the predicate non-sargable, so the B-tree index on that column cannot be used and the engine falls back to a scan. Rewrite `YEAR(d) = 2026` as `d >= '2026-01-01' AND d < '2027-01-01'`."},
{id:"sq23",deck:"sqlcore",q:"What is a CTE and when is it better than a subquery?",a:"A named temporary result set defined with WITH. Better when the same derived set is referenced more than once, when the query has several logical steps, or when you need recursion. It is not automatically materialised."},
{id:"sq24",deck:"sqlcore",q:"State the two parts of a recursive CTE.",a:"An anchor member producing the seed rows, then UNION ALL, then a recursive member that references the CTE itself. It must have a terminating condition or it runs to the recursion limit. MySQL requires `WITH RECURSIVE`."},
{id:"sq25",deck:"sqlcore",q:"How do you find the second highest salary, returning NULL when there is none?",a:"Wrap it: `SELECT (SELECT DISTINCT salary FROM emp ORDER BY salary DESC LIMIT 1 OFFSET 1) AS SecondHighest`. The outer SELECT guarantees one row containing NULL rather than an empty result set."},
{id:"sq26",deck:"sqlcore",q:"Self-join versus LAG for comparing consecutive rows — which is safer?",a:"LAG. A self-join on `a.id = b.id + 1` silently breaks the moment IDs are non-contiguous, which happens after any delete. LAG orders explicitly and does not care about gaps."},
{id:"sq27",deck:"sqlcore",q:"What does GROUP BY do to rows that a window function does not?",a:"GROUP BY collapses each group into ONE row. PARTITION BY computes per-group values while keeping every original row. If you need both the detail and the group aggregate side by side, you want a window."},
{id:"sq28",deck:"sqlcore",q:"Why does `LIMIT 10` without ORDER BY worry an interviewer?",a:"Row order is not guaranteed by the relational model, so the result is non-deterministic and can change between runs or after an index change. Always pair LIMIT with a deterministic ORDER BY, including a tie-break column."},

/* ---------- SQL Windows & Analytics ---------- */
{id:"sw01",deck:"sqlwin",q:"Write the full anatomy of an OVER clause.",a:"`func() OVER (PARTITION BY cols ORDER BY cols ROWS BETWEEN start AND end)`. PARTITION splits, ORDER sequences within a partition, and the frame decides which rows of that partition feed the function."},
{id:"sw02",deck:"sqlwin",q:"ROW_NUMBER versus RANK versus DENSE_RANK on values 10, 10, 9.",a:"ROW_NUMBER gives 1, 2, 3 (ties broken arbitrarily). RANK gives 1, 1, 3 (gap after the tie). DENSE_RANK gives 1, 1, 2 (no gap). Top-3-including-ties needs DENSE_RANK."},
{id:"sw03",deck:"sqlwin",q:"Why can you not filter on a window function in WHERE?",a:"Window functions are evaluated AFTER WHERE and GROUP BY in the logical order. Compute the window in a CTE or subquery, then filter in an outer query."},
{id:"sw04",deck:"sqlwin",q:"What is the DEFAULT window frame, and why is it a trap?",a:"With an ORDER BY present the default is `RANGE BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW`. RANGE groups PEER rows with equal ORDER BY values together, so a running total jumps in blocks over ties. Use `ROWS` when you mean physical rows."},
{id:"sw05",deck:"sqlwin",q:"Write a running total of daily revenue.",a:"`SUM(revenue) OVER (PARTITION BY store ORDER BY day ROWS BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW)`. Naming the frame explicitly avoids the RANGE-ties trap."},
{id:"sw06",deck:"sqlwin",q:"Write a 7-day moving average.",a:"`AVG(x) OVER (ORDER BY day ROWS BETWEEN 6 PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW)`. Note this is 7 ROWS, not 7 calendar days — if dates can be missing, join onto a generated date series first."},
{id:"sw07",deck:"sqlwin",q:"LAG and LEAD — signature and typical use.",a:"`LAG(col, offset, default) OVER (PARTITION BY p ORDER BY o)` reads backwards; LEAD reads forwards. Used for day-over-day deltas, detecting state changes, and finding consecutive rows without a self-join."},
{id:"sw08",deck:"sqlwin",q:"What does an OVER () clause with no ORDER BY compute?",a:"The aggregate over the ENTIRE partition (or the whole result set if there is no PARTITION BY), repeated on every row. This is exactly how you get percent-of-total without a second query."},
{id:"sw09",deck:"sqlwin",q:"Write percent of group total on each row.",a:"`100.0 * amount / SUM(amount) OVER (PARTITION BY category)`. The 100.0 forces float division; without it integer division truncates to zero."},
{id:"sw10",deck:"sqlwin",q:"Why does LAST_VALUE usually return the current row?",a:"Because the default frame ends at CURRENT ROW, so the 'last' value it can see is the current one. Fix it with `ROWS BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND UNBOUNDED FOLLOWING`."},
{id:"sw11",deck:"sqlwin",q:"Give the canonical top-N-per-group query.",a:"`WITH r AS (SELECT *, DENSE_RANK() OVER (PARTITION BY dept ORDER BY salary DESC) rk FROM emp) SELECT * FROM r WHERE rk <= 3`. Choose ROW_NUMBER instead if ties must be broken to exactly N rows."},
{id:"sw12",deck:"sqlwin",q:"State the gaps-and-islands grouping key.",a:"`value - ROW_NUMBER() OVER (ORDER BY value)` — consecutive runs share the same difference. Group by that key to collapse each island, then take MIN and MAX for its bounds, or COUNT for the streak length."},
{id:"sw13",deck:"sqlwin",q:"What does NTILE do, and where is it used?",a:"Divides an ordered partition into n roughly equal buckets and labels each row with its bucket number. Used for quartiles, deciles, and cohort bucketing. Bucket sizes differ by at most one when rows do not divide evenly."},
{id:"sw14",deck:"sqlwin",q:"PARTITION BY versus GROUP BY — state the difference precisely.",a:"GROUP BY reduces N rows to one row per group. PARTITION BY keeps all N rows and attaches a per-group computed value to each. Neither replaces the other."},
{id:"sw15",deck:"sqlwin",q:"How do you deduplicate keeping one row per key using a window?",a:"`ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY key ORDER BY updated_at DESC, id)` in a CTE, then filter where the number is 1. The extra id in the ORDER BY makes the tie-break deterministic."},
{id:"sw16",deck:"sqlwin",q:"How would you compute day-over-day growth rate?",a:"`(x - LAG(x) OVER (ORDER BY d)) * 1.0 / NULLIF(LAG(x) OVER (ORDER BY d), 0)`. The NULLIF guards the divide-by-zero on the first or on a zero previous day."},

/* ---------- DBMS Theory ---------- */
{id:"db01",deck:"dbms",q:"State the four ACID properties with a one-line failure example each.",a:"Atomicity — a transfer must not debit without crediting. Consistency — constraints hold before and after. Isolation — concurrent transfers must not read each other's partial state. Durability — a committed transfer survives a power cut."},
{id:"db02",deck:"dbms",q:"Name the four isolation levels and the anomaly each one permits.",a:"READ UNCOMMITTED allows dirty reads. READ COMMITTED prevents dirty reads but allows non-repeatable reads. REPEATABLE READ prevents those but allows phantoms. SERIALIZABLE prevents all three."},
{id:"db03",deck:"dbms",q:"Dirty read versus non-repeatable read versus phantom read.",a:"Dirty read — you read uncommitted data that may roll back. Non-repeatable read — you re-read the SAME row and its value changed. Phantom read — you re-run the same QUERY and new rows appeared."},
{id:"db04",deck:"dbms",q:"What is a deadlock and how do you handle one?",a:"Two transactions each hold a lock the other needs, so neither proceeds. Prevention: always acquire locks in a consistent order, keep transactions short, and use lower isolation where safe. Databases usually detect the cycle and kill the cheaper victim."},
{id:"db05",deck:"dbms",q:"Optimistic versus pessimistic locking.",a:"Pessimistic locks the row up front (`SELECT ... FOR UPDATE`) — right under high contention. Optimistic reads a version number and checks it has not changed at write time — right under low contention, since it avoids holding locks."},
{id:"db06",deck:"dbms",q:"Define 1NF, 2NF and 3NF, naming the dependency each removes.",a:"1NF — atomic values, no repeating groups. 2NF — 1NF plus no PARTIAL dependency of a non-key attribute on part of a composite key. 3NF — 2NF plus no TRANSITIVE dependency of a non-key attribute on another non-key attribute."},
{id:"db07",deck:"dbms",q:"What does BCNF require that 3NF does not?",a:"Every determinant must be a superkey. 3NF permits a non-key attribute to determine part of a candidate key; BCNF forbids it. The classic example involves overlapping composite candidate keys."},
{id:"db08",deck:"dbms",q:"Distinguish super key, candidate key, primary key and alternate key.",a:"Super key — any attribute set that uniquely identifies a row. Candidate key — a MINIMAL super key. Primary key — the candidate key you chose. Alternate keys — the candidate keys you did not choose."},
{id:"db09",deck:"dbms",q:"PRIMARY KEY versus UNIQUE constraint.",a:"PRIMARY KEY is UNIQUE plus NOT NULL, and there is exactly one per table. UNIQUE allows NULLs (usually multiple, since NULLs are not equal to each other) and a table can have many."},
{id:"db10",deck:"dbms",q:"Explain ON DELETE CASCADE, SET NULL and RESTRICT.",a:"CASCADE deletes the child rows too. SET NULL nulls the child's foreign key column, which therefore must be nullable. RESTRICT (or NO ACTION) blocks the parent delete while children exist."},
{id:"db11",deck:"dbms",q:"Clustered versus non-clustered index.",a:"A clustered index defines the PHYSICAL row order, so there can be only one per table and the leaf level IS the data. A non-clustered index is a separate structure whose leaves point back to the row, costing an extra lookup unless the index covers the query."},
{id:"db12",deck:"dbms",q:"Why do relational databases use B+ trees rather than hash indexes?",a:"B+ trees keep keys ORDERED, so they serve range scans, prefix matches, ORDER BY and MIN/MAX. Hash indexes give `O(1)` equality but cannot do ranges at all. B+ trees also keep all data in leaves linked as a list for efficient scans."},
{id:"db13",deck:"dbms",q:"What is the leftmost prefix rule?",a:"A composite index on (a, b, c) can serve predicates on a, on (a, b), or on (a, b, c) — but NOT on b alone or on (b, c). Column order in a composite index is a design decision, not a formality."},
{id:"db14",deck:"dbms",q:"What is a covering index?",a:"An index that contains every column the query needs, so the engine answers entirely from the index and never touches the table. In an EXPLAIN plan this shows as index-only scan or 'Using index'."},
{id:"db15",deck:"dbms",q:"Name four reasons an index is not being used.",a:"The column is wrapped in a function; a leading wildcard LIKE; the predicate is low-selectivity so a scan is cheaper; a type mismatch forces an implicit cast; or the statistics are stale."},
{id:"db16",deck:"dbms",q:"What do indexes cost?",a:"Every INSERT, UPDATE and DELETE must maintain every affected index, plus storage. Over-indexing is a real and common production problem — index for the queries you actually run."},
{id:"db17",deck:"dbms",q:"When is denormalization the right call?",a:"Read-heavy analytical workloads where join cost dominates and the data changes rarely. You trade write complexity and update anomaly risk for read speed. Say the trade-off out loud — that is what is being tested."},
{id:"db18",deck:"dbms",q:"OLTP versus OLAP.",a:"OLTP — many small concurrent read/write transactions, highly normalized, row-oriented, indexed for point lookups. OLAP — few large aggregate scans, denormalized into star or snowflake schemas, often column-oriented."},
{id:"db19",deck:"dbms",q:"Sharding versus partitioning versus replication.",a:"Partitioning splits a table within ONE database for manageability. Sharding splits data ACROSS machines for horizontal scale. Replication copies the same data to multiple nodes for read scale and failover."},
{id:"db20",deck:"dbms",q:"State the CAP theorem and what it actually forces you to choose.",a:"Under a network Partition you must choose between Consistency and Availability — you cannot have all three. It only constrains behaviour DURING a partition, which is why PACELC (adding latency-versus-consistency in normal operation) is the more useful framing."},
{id:"db21",deck:"dbms",q:"View versus materialized view.",a:"A view is a stored query, re-executed on every reference — always fresh, no storage. A materialized view stores the RESULT — fast to read but stale until refreshed. Views are updatable only under narrow conditions."},
{id:"db22",deck:"dbms",q:"What is a trigger and why are they controversial?",a:"Procedural code fired automatically on INSERT, UPDATE or DELETE. Useful for audit trails and enforcing invariants regardless of client. Controversial because they create invisible control flow that does not appear in application code."},
{id:"db23",deck:"dbms",q:"Explain SQL injection and the correct fix.",a:"User input concatenated into a query string is parsed as SQL, so input like `' OR 1=1 --` changes the query's meaning. The fix is PARAMETERISED queries or prepared statements, which send data separately from the statement. Manual escaping and blocklists are not fixes."},
{id:"db24",deck:"dbms",q:"What is a write-ahead log and what does it buy you?",a:"Changes are appended to a durable log BEFORE the data pages are written. On crash recovery the log is replayed to redo committed work and undo uncommitted work. It is how Durability and Atomicity are actually implemented."},
{id:"db25",deck:"dbms",q:"What is MVCC?",a:"Multi-Version Concurrency Control keeps multiple versions of each row, so readers see a consistent snapshot without blocking writers and writers do not block readers. It is how Postgres and InnoDB implement REPEATABLE READ."},
{id:"db26",deck:"dbms",q:"Explain a star schema.",a:"A central fact table of measures with foreign keys to surrounding denormalized dimension tables. Optimised for analytical aggregation with few joins. A snowflake schema normalizes those dimensions further, adding joins to save space."},
{id:"db27",deck:"dbms",q:"How do you approach a 'this query is slow' question?",a:"Read the EXPLAIN plan first. Look for full scans on large tables, wrong join order, and huge row estimates versus actuals. Then check: is the predicate sargable, does a usable index exist, is the join multiplying rows, and are statistics current."},
{id:"db28",deck:"dbms",q:"Stored procedure versus application-side logic.",a:"Procedures run close to the data (less network round-tripping) and centralise rules across clients. But they are hard to version-control, test and review, and they lock you to one dialect. Modern practice keeps business logic in the application."}

];
